    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Majapahit Empire was established in the late 13th century on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, arising from the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#singhasari">Singhasari</a> kingdom. Its founding is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raden-wijaya">Raden Wijaya</a> (reigned 1293–1309 CE), who leveraged alliances with Mongol forces sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> to suppress regional rivals, subsequently turning against the invaders to consolidate control. Wijaya’s political acumen, combined with the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>-growing plains of eastern Java and strategic control of trade routes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java-sea">Java Sea</a>, enabled the nascent state to secure both agricultural and commercial resources. Early consolidation involved integrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> territories and subjugating rival Javanese principalities, establishing a capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trowulan">Trowulan</a>. The geopolitical environment favored maritime expansion, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> experiencing increasing trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, and the archipelago. Under Wijaya’s successors, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a> (r. 1350–1389 CE), the empire expanded through diplomacy, marriage alliances, and military campaigns, ultimately creating one of the most powerful maritime empires in Southeast Asian history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Majapahit’s socio-economic foundation was a blend of agrarian productivity and maritime commerce. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">Rice</a> cultivation, particularly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice-terrace">terraced paddies</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, supported dense populations and provided surplus for taxation and tribute. Society was hierarchically structured, with the king and royal court at the apex, followed by nobles, religious elites, merchants, artisans, and a peasant majority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#slavery">Slavery</a> and bonded labor supplemented agricultural and construction activities, including large-scale temple and irrigation projects. Maritime trade was central to the empire’s wealth, linking ports across the archipelago, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#borneo">Borneo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, with international networks extending to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. Majapahit collected taxes through tribute systems imposed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> states, often in rice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a>, and forest products, reinforcing the central authority while supporting naval expansion. Markets and ports functioned as hubs for both domestic and international trade, facilitating cultural exchange and integrating diverse ethnic communities into the imperial economy. This dual reliance on land-based agriculture and maritime commerce underpinned Majapahit’s longevity and regional influence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Majapahit military was a hybrid force capable of both terrestrial and naval operations, reflecting the empire’s dual agrarian-maritime character. Land forces included infantry drawn from peasant levies and elite warrior retainers, armed with spears, bows, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">War elephants</a>, though less central than in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, were occasionally employed for ceremonial and strategic purposes. The naval armada, crucial to control over the archipelago, consisted of large ships (jong) capable of transporting troops, horses, and supplies over long distances, enabling rapid response to insurrections or foreign threats. Major campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gajah-mada">Gajah Mada</a>, the prime minister and military strategist during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayam-wuruk">Hayam Wuruk</a>’s reign, aimed at subjugating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#borneo">Borneo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bali">Bali</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>, consolidating tributary states through both diplomacy and coercion. Military organization was closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> system, with regional lords contributing forces in exchange for recognition and protection. The combination of disciplined infantry, elite cavalry, and a formidable navy allowed Majapahit to maintain hegemony over the Indonesian archipelago and resist external incursions from neighboring polities and Chinese fleets.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Majapahit’s governance was centralized around the king, whose authority was reinforced through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#devaraja">devaraja</a> ideology, positioning him as a divine ruler and intermediary between human and spiritual realms. Administrative oversight relied on a bureaucratic hierarchy of ministers, provincial governors, and local chieftains who collected taxes, enforced law, and mobilized military resources. Legal systems were based on a combination of customary (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adat">adat</a>) law and Javanese royal decrees, often codified in inscriptions and edicts, while religious authority played a key role in conflict resolution and societal regulation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">Vassal</a> states retained a degree of autonomy but were expected to recognize central authority, remit tribute, and supply military support. The empire’s administrative sophistication enabled the coordination of large-scale infrastructure projects, including irrigation, temples, and port facilities, while maintaining cohesion across a vast maritime network. This structure promoted both political stability and social stratification, allowing Majapahit to integrate diverse ethnic, linguistic, and religious communities while projecting influence over distant territories through a combination of centralized oversight and delegated regional governance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Majapahit exerted substantial influence over maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, shaping political, economic, and cultural patterns throughout the archipelago. Its control over strategic ports facilitated the flow of goods, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spice-trade">spices</a>, textiles, gold, and forest products, linking regional economies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, and beyond. Majapahit culture, language, and administrative practices spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> states, leaving enduring legacies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bali">Bali</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. The empire’s encouragement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> traditions fostered artistic and architectural achievements, including monumental temples and palace complexes, which influenced subsequent Javanese polities. Diplomatic and military engagement established tributary relationships that stabilized regional trade networks and deterred potential aggressors. By projecting a model of maritime supremacy coupled with centralized governance, Majapahit shaped the political imagination of Southeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, creating patterns of statecraft and commerce that persisted in successor kingdoms and facilitated the transmission of cultural and religious ideas across the archipelago.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The decline of the Majapahit Empire began in the late 14th century, accelerated by internal succession disputes, weakening central authority, and challenges from rising regional powers. Economic shifts, including the redirection of trade toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#strait-of-malacca">Malacca Strait</a> and the growing influence of Islamic sultanates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumatra">Sumatra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#java">Java</a>, reduced the empire’s fiscal base. Civil unrest, combined with pressures from rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> states and external threats such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#demak-sultanate">Sultanate of Demak</a>, undermined political cohesion. By the early 16th century, the capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#trowulan">Trowulan</a> lost prominence, with administrative centers relocating and the empire’s control over distant territories fragmenting. Cultural and religious transformations, including the gradual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamization">Islamization</a> of coastal regions, eroded the ideological foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> court. Despite sporadic attempts at restoration, Majapahit ceased to function as a unified imperial entity, with its territories absorbed by emergent sultanates. The empire’s decline illustrates the vulnerability of maritime states to both internal factionalism and shifting trade networks.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary">Majapahit was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>'s greatest empire, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> maritime power that claimed suzerainty over most of the modern Indonesian archipelago.</p>

